{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Final rebar-debug report after Gerrit 1806 merged as commit 7d908fbbc6da12f8e274b3ef1e3a2357377799ef.\n\nProblem — determine whether moving the unchanged macOS pytest trees to an APFS RAM disk reduces Gerrit Verify critical-path and runner time without changing fidelity.\n\nProven result and classification — the storage hypothesis is rejected for this workload. The implementation followed maintained OSS precedent from Apple Git CI, Aeron, and Git performance guidance, but it was an optimization experiment rather than a product defect. Rebar measurements override that directional precedent: the exact no-RAM control 31868300174 ran the default suite in 17m26s and the macOS job in 20m36s; RAM-disk treatments ran the same default suite in 18m43.77s, 20m54.61s, 18m26s, and, on final green patchset-4 run 31873067707, 20m00.60s. Every treatment was slower than control and none met the predeclared retention oracle.\n\nProof — treatment runs 31869753711, 31869919362, 31870391571, and 31873067707 independently falsified the prediction that storage placement would reduce wall time. Recursive observability/release also added 71-98 seconds; the final live run spent about 88 seconds between the 20m00.60s default result and integration start. The evidence rejects runner-temp filesystem latency as the dominant explanation and rejects retaining the RAM-disk lifecycle.\n\nTests and secondary repairs — the first treatment exposed intentionally owner-nonwritable workflow snapshot fixtures that made ordinary recursive basetemp cleanup raise PermissionError. The held-out subprocess oracle in tests/unit/test_ci_snapshot_fixture_cleanup.py was RED without per-test teardown, GREEN with teardown, RED again under the exact-defect mutation, then GREEN after restoration. Patchset 3 exposed a separate nested-pytest cacheprovider leak; exact outer -p no:cacheprovider reproduction was RED, adding the same flag to the nested process was GREEN, removing it returned RED, and restoring it returned GREEN. Focused snapshot/isolation tests passed 18 tests.\n\nFix — merged change 1806 removes the RAM-disk setup, reporting, recursive release, and detach lifecycle; restores both unchanged pytest tiers to runner.temp; removes the experiment-only workflow contract; and retains only the test-local snapshot cleanup plus held-out regression.\n\nScope and safety — selectors, markers, assertions, xdist worker counts/distribution, timeouts, Python/platform matrix, coverage-once behavior, integration tier, and collection remain unchanged. No dependency was added and no existing assertion was weakened. Merged workflow lines 651 and 666 route distinct default and integration basetemps under runner.temp.\n\nSiblings — no other production sibling was found. The only observed cleanup siblings were the snapshot permission contract and nested cacheprovider leak, both fixed in scope. Workflow parity is not applicable beyond the single matrix workflow; Ubuntu remained byte-for-byte on runner.temp throughout the experiment.\n\nResidual risk and disposition — accepted: final run 31873067707 necessarily executed the trusted-main RAM-disk workflow, so the restored no-RAM path needs several organic post-merge full runs before any 20-30 minute average claim. This remains tracked by parent runtime ticket inedible-subsimious-brahmancow. The N=3000 import long pole remains separately tracked by ravenous-beribboned-walleye.\n\nReplication and gates — run 31873067707 passed all four full suites and the exact hosted macOS cleanup path that previously failed; macOS completed in 24m42s, the workflow concluded success, LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1 were present, zero unresolved comments remained, and Gerrit 1806 merged at 2026-08-15T08:20:32Z. The merged tree contains no RAM-disk lifecycle and both basetemps are back under runner.temp."},"env_id":"0993f6a8-7a6a-4707-a671-c5bbb2691444","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786782129305693001,"uuid":"f13da055-ed07-4d16-a088-5ef99f5e0bff"}